lesson art-warm-and-cool v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=1 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Warm and cool"
  goal "Sort colours and mixes into warm and cool, work out which paint turns a mix cool, and use both in one painting."

  look {
    scene 16x15 {
      colourwheel wheel segments=12 blank=[] ring=-1 labels=true split="warm" at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      Reds, oranges and yellows are warm colours, the colours of fire and the sun. Greens, blues and
      purples are cool, the colours of water and shade. Warm colours do not make a picture hot; they
      make it feel warm.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.warm-or-cool count=1 seed=1
    practice art.which-is-warm count=2 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked art.cool-with t="red" p=0 level=medium
    }
    practice art.cool-with count=3 seed=3
    practice art.paint-for-weather count=2 hard-count=1 seed=1
    level hard {
      practice art.warm-purple count=1 seed=1
    }
    show art.purple-depends
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.warm-purple p=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.warm-sun-cool-sea
  }
  remember {
    say "Warm colours are the colours of fire and the sun, cool ones the colours of water and shade. A mix leans towards whichever paint there is more of."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The questions are marked by the machine, which judges each mix by where it sits on the colour
    wheel and refuses a colour too near the line between warm and cool to be fair. The first ones
    name a pot's colour as warm or cool. The later ones ask about mixes the child has to make in
    their head first, and one turns the question round: which paint, added to yellow, makes a cool
    colour. A common mix-up is thinking warm colours change the temperature; it is worth saying
    plainly that they change how a picture feels.

    The two-star question shows that the same two paints can make a warm or a cool colour: a
    purple with much more red in it leans warm. The painting is the three-star question, because
    the child has to place a warm thing in the cool sea and a cool thing in the warm sky.

    Respond by talking about feeling: "The sea is all blues and greens, and it makes the sun look
    even warmer." Ask where the warm and the cool meet, and what happens there.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the mix to call warm or cool comes from warm-looking pots, the warm mixes are as written, a cool colour from red is mixed once in the teacher's pen before cool colours from white, the sky paint is for a snowy morning, purple is as written, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the mix to call warm or cool is cool from a warm-looking pot, the warm mixes are as written, the cool colours are mixed from red, the sky paint is for a hot desert and the second one makes way for a second warm purple to find, purple is as written, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
}
